Meet the cowboy who never clocks out

Livestock on the loose? Call this guy

BY KATHERINE LAIDLAW (https://x.com/klaidlaw?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-9FByEs8GIwnXQ=
zcZ9_fcyoT899LZbdBOVRdQ0CjcG7TsdkcL5WtP6Vl1ePxlWqU9fGc19kYqmo2_KRMpnOZ1S4T1=
0dFQ&_hsmi=3D395358112 )

One summer, early on in his career, Illinois cattle catcher Wesley Bush got=
 a call from down in Tennessee. He=E2=80=99d just taken over the business f=
ull-time from an old hand cattle herder, and he was still finding his own f=
ooting.

The guy explained that, eight years ago, he=E2=80=99d bought a piece of pro=
perty knowing it came with a herd of cattle that roamed wild. But now he wa=
nted to sell, and the prospective buyer had a stipulation: he needed to dea=
l with the runaway cattle problem. He=E2=80=99d hired several other people =
to come round them up, but no one could get the job done.

Bush loaded up his Dodge Ram one-ton pickup and hit the road. He traveled a=
lone, just him, his dogs, and his horse behind him in a trailer. =E2=80=9CB=
uddy, this ain=E2=80=99t Illinois. These are the mountains in Tennessee,=E2=
=80=9D his client told him.

=E2=80=9CI called up a friend in Kentucky, he met me there,=E2=80=9D Bush s=
ays. =E2=80=9CWe were done by 1 o=E2=80=99clock.=E2=80=9D

Wesley Bush on assignment. (Facebook)

Bush is one of America=E2=80=99s few remaining on-call cowboys. He=E2=80=99=
s traveled as far south as Tennessee, across Ohio and Kentucky, and north t=
o Minnesota and Michigan, all in the name of catching cattle. He=E2=80=99s =
on call 24 hours a day, seven days a week.

Horsing around

Born and raised in Morrison, Illinois, Bush always imagined he=E2=80=99d wo=
rk with horses. At 13, he expressed interest in learning to rope. He spent =
a few years training his roping arm and competing in the rodeo.

=E2=80=9CI could catch cattle for friends and neighbors who needed help, as=
 long as they were visible, in pasture,=E2=80=9D he says. =E2=80=9CDuring t=
he summer, if they were in the corn, I wasn=E2=80=99t any help.=E2=80=9D

If the job required figuring out where the cattle had gone, they had to cal=
l Chet Peugh (https://www.facebook.com/WildCattleCatcher/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-=
9FByEs8GIwnXQzcZ9_fcyoT899LZbdBOVRdQ0CjcG7TsdkcL5WtP6Vl1ePxlWqU9fGc19kYqmo2=
_KRMpnOZ1S4T10dFQ&_hsmi=3D395358112 ) , a local man in the next town over r=
unning a second-generation cattle-catching business.

Chet Peugh, a second-generation cattle wrangler, sold Bush his business and=
 has since (mostly) retired. (Facebook)

Bush, in the meantime, heeded his family=E2=80=99s advice and went off to g=
et a college degree. He tried, but it didn=E2=80=99t stick and he dropped o=
ut. He worked for horse trainers for a while, then on feedlots and sale bar=
ns.

Twelve years later, he moved home to Morrison and happened to run into Peug=
h, the very guy he knew as a cattle-catcher.

He asked if he needed some help. =E2=80=9CWhere=E2=80=99s your horse?=E2=80=
=9D Peugh asked him.

Turns out it was five miles away, and Peugh told him to go get it. That cha=
nce encounter changed the course of Bush=E2=80=99s career. Together, the pa=
ir worked to round up rogue steeds for five years, until Peugh was assured =
Bush was qualified to run the business on his own.

When he bought the business from Peugh, it came with a couple of crucial it=
ems.

=E2=80=9CI got his phone number, which is the same phone number that his da=
d had. Anyone who needed cattle caught 50 years ago might have tried the ph=
one number hoping he=E2=80=99d still answer, and he didn=E2=80=99t but I wo=
uld,=E2=80=9D Bush says.

=E2=80=9CKeep that number and you=E2=80=99ll be taken care of,=E2=80=9D Peu=
gh would tell his customers.

Bush travels with a team of between three and six dogs who track the cattle=
 by scent. (Facebook)

The second was a team of dogs trained to track and find cattle, thicket of =
corn stalks be damned. (The job comes with a special kind of R&D: Bush now =
breeds his own dogs =E2=80=93 part catahoula, terrier, and hound =E2=80=93 =
to ensure he=E2=80=99s working with an expert team of trackers.)

The dogs join Bush on his cattle calls, and if they can catch a fresh scent=
, they point him in the right direction. From there, it=E2=80=99s a matter =
of rounding them up. Usually, he says, a drove on the run will split into g=
roups of 15 or less.

Cost per head

Cattle ranchers and farmers in the US aren=E2=80=99t having the best time. =
For one, the herd size is constricting: last year, it hit its lowest point =
(https://www.fb.org/market-intel/u-s-cattle-inventory-smallest-in-73-years?=
_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-9FByEs8GIwnXQzcZ9_fcyoT899LZbdBOVRdQ0CjcG7TsdkcL5WtP6Vl1eP=
xlWqU9fGc19kYqmo2_KRMpnOZ1S4T10dFQ&_hsmi=3D395358112 ) since 1951. Producti=
on costs are up, reaching record highs, and tariffs have squeezed supplies =
like fertilizer, pesticides, and tractors and other self-propelled machiner=
y.

Dairy farmers are even testing out new revenue streams, like cow cuddling (=
https://www.washingtonpost.com/lifestyle/2025/11/19/cow-cuddling-dairy-farm=
s/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-9FByEs8GIwnXQzcZ9_fcyoT899LZbdBOVRdQ0CjcG7TsdkcL5WtP6Vl=
1ePxlWqU9fGc19kYqmo2_KRMpnOZ1S4T10dFQ&_hsmi=3D395358112 ) , to boost a year=
 where prices and demand for dairy have fallen. (Surprised? Bookings for th=
e experience at one Washington County, Penn., farm (https://www.axios.com/l=
ocal/pittsburgh/2025/03/21/highland-cow-hugging-farm-pa?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-9F=
ByEs8GIwnXQzcZ9_fcyoT899LZbdBOVRdQ0CjcG7TsdkcL5WtP6Vl1ePxlWqU9fGc19kYqmo2_K=
RMpnOZ1S4T10dFQ&_hsmi=3D395358112 ) went from ~500 to 2.5k+ last year.)

Bush=E2=80=99s pricing follows the market. He quotes his clients a standard=
 fee =E2=80=93 by the head =E2=80=93 and a mileage fee to account for his t=
ravel.

=E2=80=9CDuring Covid, cattle prices dropped drastically and I dropped my p=
rices,=E2=80=9D he says. =E2=80=9CI currently get about $400 a head on them=
.=E2=80=9D

Olivia Heller/The Hustle

Still, there are always exceptions. One recent job was 96 head (or cattle) =
and he had it done in a day, so he cut the guy a deal.

=E2=80=9CPeople say I don=E2=80=99t charge enough,=E2=80=9D he says. =E2=80=
=9CI=E2=80=99m amazed at how many people give me a substantial tip.=E2=80=
=9D

Click of the gate

Bush shares ten acres outside of Morrison, Illinois, with his wife and thei=
r six-year-old son, plus eight dogs, a few cattle, and a handful of horses.=
 He estimates 90% of his business comes from the phone number he inherited =
from Peugh, and the rest from his connections across the Midwestern rodeo s=
cene and a smattering of Facebook advertising. His competitive advantage? H=
e=E2=80=99s always on call.

In the winter, on average Bush gets a call once every week or two. When the=
 cattle get out, there aren=E2=80=99t many places to hide or food to scroun=
ge up. Most of the creeks are frozen over. So the owners can usually round =
them up themselves.

But during the summer, when the crops come up, Bush is taking on two to thr=
ee jobs a day, only breaking midday to give the dogs a rest from the punish=
ing heat. =E2=80=9CI=E2=80=99ll go anywhere the phone rings from,=E2=80=9D =
he says.

Calls come in at all hours, from hobby farmers and ranching conglomerates a=
like, and within 30 minutes Bush can be out the door and on his way, priori=
tizing jobs that see cattle meeting highways or putting people or themselve=
s in danger. He likes to get where he=E2=80=99s going by daybreak =E2=80=93=
 5am in the summer =E2=80=93 which can mean a 1am wake-up after getting hom=
e at 9pm or 10pm the night before.

=E2=80=9CDuring the summer, there=E2=80=99s a lot of short nights,=E2=80=9D=
 he says.

Olivia Heller/The Hustle

And while the job itself comes with some risks (Bush=E2=80=99s list of inju=
ries starts with a broken leg and ends with a shattered shoulder blade), he=
